==Round of 16==

===Uruguay vs South Korea===
[[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]] vs [[South Korea national football team|South Korea]] was the first match in the Round of 16. The match was held at the [[Nelson Mandela Bay Stadium]] in [[Port Elizabeth]] before a low crowd of 30,597.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5hUONGwboBiMunFqECosWJ6-AQhEQ|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100702133940/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5hUONGwboBiMunFqECosWJ6-AQhEQ|url-status=dead|archive-date=2 July 2010|title=Low crowd causes concern in Port Elizabeth|date=28 June 2010|agency=[[Agence France-Presse]]|access-date=30 June 2010}}</ref> Uruguay won the match 2–1 with two goals from [[Luis Suárez]], the second of which broke a 1–1 deadlock in the 80th minute. Suárez's first was scored when [[Diego Forlán]] made a low cross from the left that was not dealt with by the South Korean defence, leaving Suárez to score at the back post. Uruguay subsequently adopted a defensive posture, and South Korea had more chances to score. Eventually, [[Lee Chung-yong]] equalised in the 68th minute, scoring a headed goal following a free kick. Despite South Korea then having chances to win the match, Suárez scored Uruguay's winner in the 80th minute with a curling strike from the edge of the 18-yard box that went in off the inside of the post; this goal was regarded as one of the tournament's best.<ref name="BBCUruguayKorea">{{cite news|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_49/default.stm|title=Uruguay 2–1 South Korea|last=Chowdhury|first=Saj|date=26 June 2010|publisher=BBC News|access-date=2 July 2010}}</ref> Korea missed more good chances in the final minutes of the game, giving Uruguay victory and passage to the quarter-finals for the first time since 1970, where they would face [[Ghana national football team|Ghana]].<ref name="BBCUruguayKorea"/><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5i0kw0Ykj_quQko06E86XJRwq9AiwD9GJ702G0|title=Uruguay beats South Korea 2–1 in round of 16|last=Ritter|first=Karl|date=26 June 2010|agency=Associated Press|access-date=30 June 2010}}{{dead link|date=June 2024|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> After the match, Uruguayan coach [[Óscar Tabárez]] attributed his team's successful run to the number of players with experience at top-level overseas clubs.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup-2010/teams/uruguay/7858926/Uruguay-glory-down-to-overseas-exploits.html|archive-url=https://archive.today/20120911223925/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup-2010/teams/uruguay/7858926/Uruguay-glory-down-to-overseas-exploits.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=11 September 2012|title=Uruguay glory down to overseas exploits|date=28 June 2010|work=[[The Daily Telegraph]]|access-date=30 June 2010 |location=London}}</ref> South Korean coach [[Huh Jung-moo]] claimed his side "controlled" the match and that Uruguay's goals were "lucky".<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.goal.com/en/news/1863/world-cup-2010/2010/06/26/1996911/world-cup-2010-south-korea-coach-huh-jung-moo-rues-uruguays|title=South Korea Coach Huh Jung-Moo Rues Uruguay's 'Lucky' Goals|last=Mira|first=Luis|date=26 June 2010|work=goal.com|access-date=2 July 2010}}</ref>

