===Brazil vs Chile===
[[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] soundly defeated [[Chile national football team|Chile]] 3–0 on 28 June 2010 at [[Ellis Park Stadium]], [[Johannesburg]] to progress to a quarter-final match against the [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2010/06/28/AR2010062803791.html|title=Brazil finds magic, tops Chile, 3–0, at World Cup|last=Goff|first=Steven|date=29 June 2010|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|publisher=[[Washington Post Company]]|access-date=3 July 2010}}</ref> Brazil's first goal came from a corner kick taken by [[Maicon Sisenando|Maicon]] in the 34th minute, with [[Juan (footballer, born 1979)|Juan]] heading the ball into the goal without being marked. Brazil had doubled its lead within five minutes after a free-flowing passing movement involving [[Robinho]] and [[Kaká]] that teed up [[Luís Fabiano]] to score after taking the ball around the Chilean goalkeeper. Robinho himself sealed victory for Brazil in the second half, scoring following a long run with the ball by [[Ramires]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_54/default.stm|title=Brazil 3–0 Chile|last=Fletcher|first=Paul|date=28 June 2010|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=3 July 2010 }}</ref> After the match, Chile's coach [[Marcelo Bielsa]] conceded that his team had been outplayed, arguing that in his position there was "little one can do" when up against a team of Brazil's quality.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/world-cup/story/_/id/803821/ce/uk/?cc=3436&ver=global|title=Bielsa: Brazil were the better side|date=29 June 2010|publisher=[[ESPNsoccernet]]|access-date=3 July 2010|archive-date=21 October 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121021200440/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/world-cup/story/_/id/803821/ce/uk/?cc=3436&ver=global|url-status=dead}}</ref>

