==Quarter-finals==

===Netherlands vs Brazil===
{{external media
| float  = right
| video1 = {{YouTube|XojtTwQ3REg|Netherlands v Brazil (South Africa 2010) Full match}}
}}
The [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]] versus [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] was the first quarter-final match, held on 2 July 2010 at the [[Nelson Mandela Bay Stadium]] in [[Port Elizabeth]]. The Netherlands won 2–1 after recovering from a 1–0 deficit, knocking the five-time world champions Brazil out of the tournament.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2010/07/03/2943850.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100705005251/http://www.abc.net.au/news/stories/2010/07/03/2943850.htm|url-status=dead|archive-date=5 July 2010|title=Brazil shellshocked as Dutch turn it around|last=Maasdorp|first=James|date=2 July 2010|work=[[ABC News (Australia)|ABC News]]|access-date=2 July 2010 }}</ref> The Netherlands' team was affected by an injury to [[Joris Mathijsen]] before the match. His replacement in defence – [[André Ooijer]] – and fellow central defender [[John Heitinga]] were at fault for Brazil's opening goal in the 10th minute, when [[Robinho]] was allowed to latch onto a deep pass from [[Felipe Melo]] and score without being challenged by the defence.<ref name="BBCNedBra">{{cite news|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_57/default.stm|title=Netherlands 2–1 Brazil|last=Bevan|first=Chris|date=2 July 2010|work=BBC News|access-date=2 July 2010 }}</ref><ref name="FoxNedBra">{{cite news|url=http://www.foxsports.com.au/story/0,8659,27355567-5019291,00.html|title=The Netherlands upset Brazil 2–1 in FIFA World Cup quarter-final, South Africa|last=MacKinnon|first=Angus|date=3 July 2010|work=[[Fox Sports (Australia)|Fox Sports]]|access-date=2 July 2010}}</ref> For the rest of the first half, the Dutch were largely frustrated by the Brazilian defence, and needed to rely on [[Maarten Stekelenburg]]'s goalkeeping to prevent Brazil from extending its lead. However, eight minutes after half-time, the Dutch equalised through a goal from Sneijder. The goal was initially recorded as an own goal by Felipe Melo, but [[FIFA]] overturned the decision to credit the goal to Sneijder.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/newsid=1266860/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100706084508/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/newsid=1266860/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=6 July 2010|title=Sneijder credited with first goal in Dutch quarter-final victory|date=3 July 2010|work=FIFA.com|access-date=3 July 2010 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/2010-07-03/sneijder-moves-into-tie-for-goal-scoring-lead-after-fifa-changes-credit.html|title=Sneijder Moves Into Tie for Goal-Scoring Lead After FIFA Changes Credit|last=Bensch|first=Bob|date=3 July 2010|work=[[Bloomberg L.P.]]|access-date=9 July 2010}}</ref> Having equalised, the Netherlands subsequently took the lead from a Robben corner kick in the 68th minute, Sneijder heading the ball in after a flick-on from Kuyt. Brazil's chances of restoring parity were damaged when Felipe Melo was sent off for a stamp on Robben. The Netherlands held out for the win, thereby earning passage to the semi-finals.<ref name="BBCNedBra"/> Brazil's coach [[Dunga]] confirmed after the match that he would be leaving the position upon the expiry of his contract, admitting responsibility for Brazil's defeat.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.skysports.com/football/world-cup-2010/story/0,27032,12027_6242736,00.html|title=Dunga reign at an end|date=2 July 2010|work=[[Sky Sports]]|access-date=2 July 2010 }}</ref> The Dutch coach [[Bert van Marwijk]] attacked the Brazilian team after the match, claiming they had provoked his side, and that Melo's stamp on Robben left him "ashamed for Brazilian football."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5iFJEMYc7TEcgtpZV71acUuYDTj7w|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100705105048/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5iFJEMYc7TEcgtpZV71acUuYDTj7w|url-status=dead|archive-date=5 July 2010|title=Dutch boss Van Marwijk attacks 'shameful' Brazil|date=2 July 2010|agency=[[Agence France-Presse]]|access-date=2 July 2010 }}</ref>

