===Paraguay vs Spain===
On 3 July 2010, [[Spain national football team|Spain]] defeated [[Paraguay national football team|Paraguay]] 1–0, to secure entry to the semi-finals where they would meet [[Germany national football team|Germany]]. It was the first time that Spain had progressed to the semi-final of a World Cup since 1950; while for Paraguay, the quarter-final appearance was also the country's best ever performance.<ref name="BBCSpaPar">{{cite news|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2010/matches/match_60/default.stm|title=Paraguay 0–1 Spain|last=Fletcher|first=Paul|date=3 July 2010|work=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=5 July 2010 }}</ref>
The first half of the match finished goalless, although both sides had chances to score and Paraguay's [[Nelson Haedo Valdez|Nelson Valdez]] had a goal ruled out as offside. The match suddenly became eventful in the second half due to a string of penalty kicks. First, [[Óscar Cardozo]] was pulled down by [[Gerard Piqué]] in Spain's penalty area and Paraguay was awarded a penalty. Cardozo took the penalty himself but it was saved by Spain's goalkeeper [[Iker Casillas]]. Spain soon after launched an attack at the other end of the field, in which [[David Villa]] was ruled by the referee to have been brought down by [[Antolín Alcaraz]]. [[Xabi Alonso]] stepped up to take the penalty kick and seemed to have scored, only for the referee to order it be retaken because of encroachment by a Spanish player into the penalty area before the kick was taken. Xabi Alonso's retake was saved by Paraguayan goalkeeper [[Justo Villar]]. As a result, the score remained 0–0 after the three penalty kicks. However, Spain ultimately managed to take the lead in the 82nd minute: David Villa collected a rebounded shot off the right post from [[Pedro (footballer, born July 1987)|Pedro]], to score himself with a shot that again hit the right post before going in off the left post. The goal turned out to be the winner for Spain.<ref name="BBCSpaPar"/>
After the match, Spain's coach [[Vicente del Bosque]] conceded that his side were not playing at their best and were starved of possession. He also noted his view that Spain's next opponents Germany were the best team at the World Cup.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/world_cup_2010/8786181.stm|title=Spain must improve, admits coach|date=3 July 2010|work=[[BBC Sport]]|publisher=[[British Broadcasting Corporation]]|access-date=5 July 2010 }}</ref> Paraguay's coach [[Gerardo Martino]] stated he would be leaving his position at the end of his contract.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.skysports.com/football/world-cup-2010/story/0,27032,12098_6246020,00.html|title=Martino quits Paraguay|last=Razwan Mirza|date=5 July 2010|publisher=[[Sky Sports]]|access-date=5 July 2010 }}</ref>

